#: {{syn|en|précis}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|year_published=1940|author=w:F.J. Thwaites|title={{w|The Redemption (novel)|The Redemption}}|publisher=H. John Edwards|location=Sydney|chapter=XI|page=119|text=He had scanned the '''resum&eacute;''' of the three previous instalments, and was abut to commence the story[.]}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|North America|Australia}} A summary or [[account]] of education and [[employment]] experiences and qualifications; a [[curriculum vitae]] (often for presentation to a [[potential]] future [[employer]] when applying for a job). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|curriculum vitae|CV}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The spellings {{m|en|résumé}} and {{m|en|resumé}} are preferred by dictionaries. While the spelling {{m|en|resume}} was historically more common in practice, in recent years, the spelling {{m|en|résumé}} may have become the most common.<!--This is not definitive evidence since Google NGram will give a different ranking depending on the preceding word; try replacing "his" with "their", "her", "my", "our", or "the"--><ref>{{R:GNV|his résumé,his resumé,his resume}}</ref>
* In Canada, {{m|en|resumé}} is the sole spelling given by the Canadian Oxford Dictionary; {{m|en|résumé}} is the only spelling given by the Gage Canadian Dictionary (1997 edition).
* In the US, there are three major spellings of this word: {{m|en|résumé}}, {{m|en|resumé}}, and {{m|en|resume}}. All three are in common usage and all three are occasionally contested. The usual justification for each is usually as follows:
** {{m|en|resume}} is an acceptable spelling, because modern English does not usually have diacritic marks except when borrowing terms or as an optional spelling to indicate a breach of standard pronunciation rules. Compare {{m|en|naive}}, {{m|en|emigre}}, {{m|en|nee}}, and {{m|en|fiance}}, all of which are commonly spelled with and without accent marks. The spelling ''resume'' is more likely to be found on the web due to the limits of ASCII character encoding and the US English keyboard.
** {{m|en|resumé}} follows a practice wherein a final {{m|en|e}} is accented to indicate that it is pronounced where it would usually remain silent. Compare {{m|en|touché}}, {{m|en|café}}, and especially {{m|en|saké}} and {{m|en|maté}}, where there is no etymological precedent for the accent.
** {{m|en|résumé}} follows a practice of retaining accents in borrowed words, which some may consider affected. Compare {{m|en|protégé}}, {{m|en|émigré}}, {{m|en|née}}, and {{m|en|élan}}.
* Certain other French words with two accented {{m|en|e|e's}} have the same usage conflict, though the relative infrequency of the words in common usage causes the conflict to be less pronounced. Also, some spell-checking tools prescribe against {{m|en|resumé}}, suggesting {{m|en|résumé}} instead, which may affect the perception of the correctness of the two spellings of the term.

